    <h2>The Color and the Brand Are the Distractions</h2>

    <p>Every paint conversation centers on two things: the color and the paint brand. Homeowners spend a week on swatches and ask which premium line to buy, believing that's where quality comes from. It's the wrong focus, and the paint companies are happy to keep it there because it sells buckets. The truth painters know and rarely get asked about: the finish paint is the last and least decisive variable in whether the job lasts. You're buying a surface. Paint is just what you see on top of it.</p>

    <h2>The Job Is 70 Percent Prep</h2>

    <p>Paint is a bond, and a bond is only as good as what it grabs. It won't hold to a dirty surface, a chalky one, a glossy one, or a damp one — and Cullman exteriors collect all four between pollen, humidity, and sun-chalked old paint. Real prep is a sequence: wash the surface clean, scrape and sand the failing paint to sound material, spot-prime the bare wood — bare wood without primer is where peeling starts — caulk the gaps that let water behind the paint, and let it dry before painting. That sequence is 60 to 70 percent of the labor in a quality job. When someone underbids the field by a wide margin, they're not more efficient; they're skipping steps on that list.</p>

    <h2>How a Real Painter Spends the First Day</h2>

    <p>The tell of a real painter is how much time they spend not painting. They wash and let it dry a full day instead of painting over grime. They prime stains and bare wood with the right product — an oil or stain-blocking primer over knots and water marks that latex alone bleeds through. They caulk and fill before the finish coat, not after. They watch the weather and refuse to paint an exterior into the evening dew or the dead humidity of an August afternoon, because paint that can't cure fails. The historic homes downtown, around the Warehouse District and the Festhalle, punish shortcuts hardest — original wood, heavy trim, and layers of old paint that demand patient prep. That's exactly where painting overlaps with a good <a href="/locations/cullman/pressure-washing/">pressure washing</a> crew for the soft-wash and with a <a href="/locations/cullman/general-contractor/">general contractor</a> when rotten trim has to be replaced before any paint makes sense.</p>

    <h2>The Tells in a Cheap Bid</h2>

    <p>The warning signs are consistent. A bid dramatically lower than the others and vague about prep. A crew that wants to start tomorrow with no wash day built in. "One coat will cover it" on a color change or bare wood — quality work is two coats over primed surfaces almost every time. And no mention of caulking, priming, or drying time anywhere in the quote. The honest admission that protects you: a painter who quotes a big prep number and a longer timeline is usually the one being straight, and the one promising fast-and-cheap is usually pricing a job that looks great for a year and peels in the second.</p>

    <h2 id="pricing-summary">What Painting Costs in Cullman</h2>

    <p>Real ranges:</p>
    <ul class="trades-list-block">
      <li><strong>Interior painting</strong> — about $2 to $4 per square foot of floor area, more with high ceilings, heavy trim, or wall repair.</li>
      <li><strong>Exterior repaint</strong> — roughly $3,000 to $7,000 for a typical single-story, driven by prep condition.</li>
      <li><strong>Historic downtown exteriors</strong> — higher, for the exacting prep and heavier trim detail.</li>
      <li><strong>Trim, doors, and cabinets</strong> — priced separately; fine finish work is slower and more skilled than wall painting.</li>
    </ul>

    <p>The spread on exteriors is almost entirely prep. Two honest painters can quote the same house differently because one plans to do the scraping the other plans to skip.</p>

    <h2>Ask One Question Before You Sign</h2>

    <p>Ask every bidder to walk you through their prep. The painter who answers in detail — wash, dry, scrape, sand, prime, caulk — is the one whose work will last, and the price gap between them and the fast-and-cheap crew is the difference between painting once and painting again in three years. Time exteriors for the drier, milder stretches of spring and fall, when paint can actually cure, rather than peak summer humidity — doubly true at the lake. Interior work can happen year-round.</p>

    <h2>Painting Questions Cullman Homeowners Ask</h2>

    <p><strong>Why did my exterior peel in two years?</strong></p>
    <p>Failed prep almost every time — paint over dirt, gloss, or bare wood doesn't bond. Prep is 60 to 70 percent of a real job and the part cheap bids cut.</p>

    <p><strong>Is expensive paint worth it?</strong></p>
    <p>A little; prep matters far more. Great prep with average paint outlasts premium paint over bad prep.</p>

    <p><strong>What does it cost?</strong></p>
    <p>Interior $2 to $4 a square foot; a single-story exterior repaint $3,000 to $7,000, mostly depending on prep condition.</p>

    <h2>If You Paint Houses in Cullman</h2>

    <p>Painting is the trade where trust is everything and proof is easy — before-and-after photos, a clear description of your prep process, and real reviews are the most persuasive marketing there is, and almost no painter in Cullman puts them online. So a careful painter and a spray-and-pray crew look identical on Google, and the homeowner who'd happily pay for real work can't tell you apart and defaults to whoever's cheapest or whoever an out-of-town company's page surfaced first. A website that walks through your prep and shows the work photographed honestly reaches exactly the customer who researches before hiring — the historic-home owner downtown and the lake-house owner both. The <a href="/locations/cullman/">Cullman contractor overview</a> explains why that buyer is so reachable. <a href="/">Sites On Call</a> builds the page that lets your reputation <a href="/articles/why-word-of-mouth-isnt-enough/">travel further than word of mouth</a>. If your best jobs never get seen by the next customer, let's fix that.</p>

